TMPFILE(3)                     P32W Unix                      TMPFILE(3)

NAME
       tmpfile, tmpnam, mkstemp - temporary files

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       FILE *tmpfile(void);
       char *tmpnam(char *s);

       #include <stdlib.h>

       int mkstemp(char *template);

DESCRIPTION
       tmpfile makes a new file in /tmp, opens it for reading and
       writing ("w+") and removes its name at once: the file goes
       away when the stream is closed, or when the program ends.

       mkstemp makes a new file, mode 0600, whose name is template
       with its last six characters, which must be XXXXXX, replaced
       by letters and digits; template is changed to the name. The
       file is made with O_CREAT and O_EXCL, so it is always a new
       one, even if other programs choose names at the same time.

       tmpnam returns a name in /tmp that no file has: in s, which
       must hold L_tmpnam (32) bytes, or in a static buffer if s is
       NULL. Another program could create a file of that name
       before this one does; mkstemp has no such window.

RETURN VALUE
       tmpfile returns the stream, or NULL. mkstemp returns the
       open descriptor, or -1 with errno set (EINVAL: the template
       does not end in XXXXXX). tmpnam returns the name, or NULL if
       TMP_MAX names were all in use.

EXAMPLES
       char name[] = "/tmp/sortXXXXXX";
       int fd = mkstemp(name);
       ...
       close(fd);
       unlink(name);

SEE ALSO
       fopen(3), open(2), unlink(2)
